00:00:02.470 --> 00:00:04.470 align:start position:0%
it's one of the most anticipated  activities of the year harvesting  mangoes the the sherry variety is native  to uttar pradesh in northern india  janet faridi's family has owned this  orchard for centuries he says a  scorching hot summer has destroyed at  least 30 percent of his crop  we witnessed unprecedented heat this  time the weather destroyed our crop at  all stages from flowering to ripening  even though we gave a lot of water we  didn't get the output we usually get  rainfall has also been low  south asia is the world's largest  producer of mangoes but the march peak  growing season was the hottest in a  century and average temperatures in  april were much higher than normal  traders say production is down by nearly  70 percent in india and by nearly half  in pakistan this is just one of the 1500  varieties of mangoes found across india  the summer favorite is locally called  the king of fruits and holds immense  cultural value the roles are globally  renowned but the pandemic has disrupted  the multi-million dollar trade and this  year exports are expected to decline  even further  less produce has pushed up prices and  sellers say inflation has also impacted  their incomes  we are able to sell but the business is  not as profitable even the farmers say  they are not making as much money this  year the season is about to end in about  20 days we have stock but the prices are  much higher  experts say rapid urbanization in the  region has also degraded orchards and  climate change is making them even more  vulnerable when we talk about solutions  we are actually talking about  adaptability  and in pakistan unfortunately we do not  have a national adaptation plan which  has to be in place for us  to kind of introduce climate smart  agriculture techniques so that we can  actually  cope up with the changing environment  this also calls for capacity building of  local farmers so that they can adapt to  the changing environment  global warming is expected to make  extreme weather events more common and  intense and junaid faridi says he can  only hope the government will do more to  help farmers like him pav nimittal  al-jazeera new delhi
 

